1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:01,853
Welcome to slideSonnet.

2
00:00:01,853 --> 00:00:06,985
Everything you're about to hear was written as plain text in a sidecar file,

3
00:00:06,985 --> 00:00:09,213
and rendered straight from a PDF.

4
00:00:09,213 --> 00:00:10,563
Let me show you how.

5
00:00:11,963 --> 00:00:14,169
So what does slideSonnet actually do?

6
00:00:15,069 --> 00:00:17,717
You bring a finished PDF of your slides,

7
00:00:17,717 --> 00:00:20,828
and slideSonnet turns it into a narrated video.

8
00:00:20,828 --> 00:00:25,196
The narration you're hearing right now lives in a plain text file,

9
00:00:25,196 --> 00:00:27,248
sitting right next to that PDF.

10
00:00:28,148 --> 00:00:31,341
Why not just put the narration inside the slides themselves?

11
00:00:32,241 --> 00:00:35,025
Because the PDF is your frozen artifact.

12
00:00:35,025 --> 00:00:37,252
Keeping narration in a separate,

13
00:00:37,252 --> 00:00:42,263
git-diffable file means you can rewrite a line and re-render in seconds.

14
00:00:42,263 --> 00:00:46,161
No recompiling the slides, no re-recording a microphone.

15
00:00:47,061 --> 00:00:48,898
Step one: mark your slides.

16
00:00:48,898 --> 00:00:52,300
Add the slideSonnet package to your Beamer source,

17
00:00:52,300 --> 00:00:56,655
and give every slide a stable id with the ess-ess-eye-dee macro.

18
00:00:56,655 --> 00:01:01,759
The id gets stamped invisibly onto each page, so narration binds to the id,

19
00:01:01,759 --> 00:01:05,637
not to a page number that shifts when you insert a slide.

20
00:01:06,537 --> 00:01:08,718
Step two: scaffold the sidecar.

21
00:01:08,718 --> 00:01:11,533
Run slidesonnet init on your PDF, and it

22
00:01:11,533 --> 00:01:14,488
reads the ids straight out of the document

23
00:01:14,488 --> 00:01:17,162
and writes a blank narration file, one

24
00:01:17,162 --> 00:01:20,117
block per slide, ready for you to fill in.

25
00:01:20,117 --> 00:01:21,665
By hand, or by an LLM.

26
00:01:22,565 --> 00:01:26,992
The format is simple. An at-sign and the slide id starts a block.

27
00:01:26,992 --> 00:01:31,693
Optional voice and pace directives go on lines starting with a colon.

28
00:01:31,693 --> 00:01:35,575
And square-bracket pause, with a number, inserts silence.

29
00:01:35,575 --> 00:01:39,117
That one primitive covers a pause between sentences,

30
00:01:39,117 --> 00:01:43,068
a hold at the end of a slide, or an entirely silent slide.

31
00:01:43,968 --> 00:01:46,604
Do I really have to write that text file by hand?

32
00:01:47,504 --> 00:01:51,445
You can, or you can run slidesonnet edit for a local editor.

33
00:01:51,445 --> 00:01:55,123
Page through the deck, type narration beside each slide,

34
00:01:55,123 --> 00:01:56,963
and preview the whole thing.

35
00:01:56,963 --> 00:01:59,130
The preview bakes in your pauses,

36
00:01:59,130 --> 00:02:02,480
so it plays back exactly like the final video will.

37
00:02:03,380 --> 00:02:04,719
Step four: render.

38
00:02:04,719 --> 00:02:09,703
Run slidesonnet export with the piper engine for free local speech,

39
00:02:09,703 --> 00:02:12,976
or elevenlabs for high quality cloud voices.

40
00:02:12,976 --> 00:02:14,612
Want a fast rough cut?

41
00:02:14,612 --> 00:02:19,149
Use silent mode, or estimate timing, to skip speech entirely.

42
00:02:19,149 --> 00:02:23,315
Subtitles are written alongside the video automatically.

43
00:02:24,215 --> 00:02:26,363
Doesn't re-rendering everything get slow?

44
00:02:27,263 --> 00:02:29,291
Audio is cached by content.

45
00:02:29,291 --> 00:02:35,301
Edit one block, and only that line is re-synthesized. Everything else is reused.

46
00:02:35,301 --> 00:02:38,231
And swapping piper for elevenlabs never

47
00:02:38,231 --> 00:02:41,462
throws away the audio you already paid for.

48
00:02:42,362 --> 00:02:44,131
That's the whole workflow.

49
00:02:44,131 --> 00:02:48,826
Mark your slides, scaffold the narration, check that the ids line up,

50
00:02:48,826 --> 00:02:50,935
write and preview, then export.

51
00:02:50,935 --> 00:02:54,950
Every step is scriptable, so an LLM or a Makefile can drive

52
00:02:54,950 --> 00:02:58,488
the entire pipeline without ever opening the editor.

53
00:02:59,388 --> 00:03:04,995
Mark your slides. Write narration. Render. Give slideSonnet a try.
